Fillets: a rounding whose two faces come tangent at the vertex where it
ends vanishes there, its contacts meeting at the vertex (mitcad#236). Where
the faces are tangent there only to within a few micrometres (e.g. an arch
whose radius misses the walls it springs from by a micrometre, a stored
face), or the walk stops a little short of the vertex where the sections
get singular, the stripe was left with an end section of micrometres off
the vertex, and the corner at the vertex failed (the end face's
intersection with the blend) or gave an invalid result (a cap of
micrometres whose wire intersects itself).

- ChFi3d_ShrinkVanishingEnd, called by PerformFilletOnVertex for a
  constant-radius stripe ending alone at the vertex: where both contact
  points of the stripe's end lie within a hundredth of the radius of the
  vertex, both faces of the end's surface data hold the vertex and their
  normals there are less than 0.002 rad from parallel, and the contact
  curves are
  B-splines ending there (a walked blend), the end of the contact curves
  (in 3d and on the faces) is moved to the vertex (their end poles), their
  tolerance raised to cover the blend surface's own end, and both common
  points of the end become the vertex, on the arcs that hold it at its
  parameter. The stripe's end is then degenerate, and the corner is the
  singular one (PerformSingularCorner): the blend ends in a point at the
  vertex, with a degenerated edge, as where the walk reaches it exactly.
- ChFi3d_BSplineEndingAt: the non-periodic B-spline a curve is or trims,
  and the index of its pole at a given end.
